{
  "id": "chain-rule-on-path",
  "title": "Chain Rule on a Path: Gradient Dot Velocity",
  "eyebrow": "multivariable chain rule",
  "language": "en",
  "duration": 55,
  "topic": "The multivariable chain rule visualised. A particle moves along a parametric curve γ(t) on a contour map of f. As it crosses level curves the altitude z = f(γ(t)) rises and falls; the instantaneous rate dz/dt equals the gradient of f dotted with the velocity γ'(t). Concrete example: f(x, y) = x² + y², γ(t) = (cos t + 1, sin t). The particle traces a unit circle offset right; the live dot product ∇f · γ' tracks against the analytic value −2 sin t.",
  "subject_id": "mat2b",
  "chapter_number": 5,
  "prerequisites": [
    "gradient-and-level-curves",
    "directional-derivative"
  ],
  "beats": [
